- Retrieved practices:
- Avoid invalid pointer-to-member access in C++
WHAT: Using .* or ->* with a pointer-to-member that refers to a member not present in the dynamic type of the object, or using a null pointer-to-member, causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable security vulnerabilities.
HOW: Ensure the dynamic type of the object actually defines the targeted member before using .* or ->*; never use null pointer-to-member values; avoid casting pointer-to-member values across unrelated types.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `void (Base::*pm)() = nullptr; (obj.*pm)();` After (safe): `void (Base::*pm)() = &Base::f; if (pm) (obj.*pm)();`

- Detect errors when converting a string to a number in C++ streams
WHAT: Failing to detect conversion errors from std::cin (e.g., non-numeric or out-of-range input) can leave variables uninitialized or set to unintended values while code continues execution.
WHY: Using uninitialized or incorrect values can cause undefined behavior, logic errors, or security vulnerabilities such as incorrect authorization or corrupted state.
HOW: Enable exceptions on std::cin for failbit and badbit, wrap input operations in try/catch, and treat any std::istream::failure as an error that must be handled before using the read variables.
EXAMPLE:
Before: `int i,j; std::cin >> i >> j; // i/j may be invalid or uninitialized`
After: `int i,j; std::cin.exceptions(std::istream::failbit|std::istream::badbit); try { std::cin >> i >> j; } catch (std::istream::failure&) { /* handle error */ }`

- Do not access non-value bits of an object’s representation
WHAT: Accessing or modifying bits of an object’s representation that are not part of its value representation (for example, padding bits or internal control structures like vtable pointers) can lead to undefined behavior and object corruption.
WHY: Such undefined behavior can corrupt program state, break type and memory safety, and enable attacks such as control-flow hijacking via corrupted vtables or object layouts.
HOW: Only read non-value bits when their values are not used or assumed, and only write to padding bits; never write to bits that may contain control data (for example, vtable pointers). Prefer C++ special member functions and overloaded operators over low-level C library functions when copying or moving objects.
EXAMPLE (C++): Instead of manually manipulating object bytes, use safe copying: `S s2 = s1;` or define copy/move constructors and assignment operators; if `std::memcpy` is used, ensure it only copies trivially copyable objects and does not rely on or modify non-value bits beyond padding.

- Prevent data races on adjacent C++ bit-fields in multithreaded code
WHAT: Concurrent writes to different C++ bit-fields in the same storage unit (for example, adjacent bit-fields in a struct) from multiple threads cause data races and undefined behavior.
WHY: Data races can lead to unpredictable behavior, corrupted state, and exploitable memory-safety vulnerabilities that are hard to diagnose.
HOW: Do not access adjacent bit-fields from multiple threads without synchronization; either protect all accesses to the containing object with the same mutex, or separate bit-fields by inserting a non–bit-field member so each bit-field occupies its own storage unit.
EXAMPLE:
Before: struct S { unsigned f1:2; unsigned f2:2; }; // f1 and f2 may share storage and be written in different threads.
After: struct S { unsigned f1:2; unsigned pad; unsigned f2:2; }; // or guard all accesses to S with a single shared mutex.

- Do not access an object outside of its lifetime
WHAT: Accessing or copying into objects whose lifetime has not started (for example, memory returned by an allocator where constructors have not been run) causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Using objects outside their lifetime can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable undefined behavior.
HOW: After allocating raw storage (e.g., from get_temporary_buffer, malloc, operator new[] without initialization), explicitly construct objects (e.g., via placement new, container/emplace APIs) before use, and destroy them before deallocation.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `auto buf = std::get_temporary_buffer<S>(n); std::copy(src_begin, src_end, buf.first);`
After (safe): `auto buf = std::get_temporary_buffer<S>(n); for (ptr=buf.first; ptr!=buf.first+n; ++ptr) new (ptr) S(); std::copy(src_begin, src_end, buf.first);`



